WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.873
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level four.

2
00:00:06.173 --> 00:00:09.617
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.917 --> 00:00:16.285
Unit twenty-five: AI and Global Rules. Today you will express duties and freedoms in regulation.

4
00:00:16.585 --> 00:00:19.121
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:23.508
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:26.071
Artificial intelligence.

7
00:00:27.083 --> 00:00:32.868
Artificial intelligence means computer systems that perform tasks normally needing human thinking.

8
00:00:33.483 --> 00:00:37.143
Artificial intelligence now translates languages instantly.

9
00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:41.285
Your turn. Say: Artificial intelligence.

10
00:00:44.133 --> 00:00:46.063
Artificial intelligence.

11
00:00:46.817 --> 00:00:48.027
Regulation.

12
00:00:49.017 --> 00:00:53.385
Regulation means an official rule or the process of controlling something by rules.

13
00:00:53.983 --> 00:00:56.378
Regulation protects users from harm.

14
00:00:56.983 --> 00:00:59.334
Your turn. Say: Regulation.

15
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:03.364
Regulation.

16
00:01:04.117 --> 00:01:05.187
Ethics.

17
00:01:06.183 --> 00:01:09.297
Ethics means moral principles about what is right and wrong.

18
00:01:09.883 --> 00:01:12.173
Ethics guides how data should be used.

19
00:01:12.783 --> 00:01:15.320
Your turn. Say: Ethics.

20
00:01:18.167 --> 00:01:19.145
Ethics.

21
00:01:19.883 --> 00:01:20.675
Risk.

22
00:01:21.683 --> 00:01:25.053
Risk means the possibility that something harmful will happen.

23
00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:27.940
The risk of bias must be tested.

24
00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:31.411
Your turn. Say: Risk.

25
00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:35.210
Risk.

26
00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:37.230
Transparency.

27
00:01:38.217 --> 00:01:42.085
Transparency means being open about how decisions and systems work.

28
00:01:42.683 --> 00:01:44.985
Transparency builds public trust.

29
00:01:45.583 --> 00:01:48.735
Your turn. Say: Transparency.

30
00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.089
Transparency.

31
00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:55.641
International cooperation.

32
00:01:56.650 --> 00:02:00.902
International cooperation means countries working together on a common problem.

33
00:02:01.517 --> 00:02:04.747
International cooperation can set common standards.

34
00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:08.943
Your turn. Say: International cooperation.

35
00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:13.741
International cooperation.

36
00:02:14.483 --> 00:02:15.566
Innovation.

37
00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:19.978
Innovation means a new idea, method or product.

38
00:02:20.583 --> 00:02:22.629
Innovation drives economic growth.

39
00:02:23.217 --> 00:02:25.823
Your turn. Say: Innovation.

40
00:02:28.667 --> 00:02:29.668
Innovation.

41
00:02:30.417 --> 00:02:31.673
Accountability.

42
00:02:32.683 --> 00:02:36.668
Accountability means being responsible for your actions and decisions.

43
00:02:37.283 --> 00:02:40.328
Accountability means someone answers for mistakes.

44
00:02:40.917 --> 00:02:44.022
Your turn. Say: Accountability.

45
00:02:46.867 --> 00:02:48.088
Accountability.

46
00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:52.922
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:53.783 --> 00:02:59.953
Visal, a draft law says 'Developers are required to publish their testing results.' How is that different from 'must'?

48
00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:13.933
'Are required to' and 'are obliged to' describe a duty that comes from a rule, so they suit regulation. 'Must' can come from the speaker. 'Should' and 'ought to' give advice, not a legal duty.

49
00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:17.050
What about prohibition and permission?

50
00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:28.154
'Must not' and 'are prohibited from + -ing' ban things. 'Are not required to' means no duty, which is different from 'must not'. 'Are permitted to' or 'may' gives permission.

51
00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:30.855
Why is the difference important?

52
00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:44.021
In regulation, a small difference changes legal responsibility. 'Need not' means optional, but 'must not' means forbidden. Writers must choose carefully to avoid loopholes.

53
00:03:45.167 --> 00:03:46.679
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:47.517 --> 00:03:53.127
Grammar focus: Obligation in rules: required to and must not and need not.

55
00:03:53.427 --> 00:03:54.625
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:55.317 --> 00:03:58.419
Developers are required to publish their testing results.

57
00:03:59.317 --> 00:04:02.036
Companies must not use AI to mislead the public.

58
00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:05.925
Small firms need not appoint an ethics officer.

59
00:04:06.867 --> 00:04:16.299
Be required to / be obliged to = a duty from a rule; must not = forbidden; need not / are not required to = optional. Should / ought to = advice.

60
00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:21.444
Be careful! Don't say: Firms must not to share the data.

61
00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:25.678
Say: Firms must not share the data.

62
00:04:25.978 --> 00:04:28.210
Use the base verb after must not.

63
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.641
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:32.433 --> 00:04:34.154
Writing Rules for Machines.

65
00:04:34.454 --> 00:04:39.902
Artificial intelligence brings enormous innovation, but it also creates new risk.

66
00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:47.985
A proposed regulation says that developers are required to explain how their systems work, and companies must not hide errors.

67
00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:53.414
Transparency means that users are obliged to be told when they are speaking to a machine.

68
00:04:54.400 --> 00:05:00.777
Ethics boards ought to include voices from many countries, and small firms need not meet the same costs as giants.

69
00:05:01.767 --> 00:05:08.074
International cooperation is essential, because accountability fails if each nation writes different rules.

70
00:05:09.167 --> 00:05:13.180
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:14.017 --> 00:05:17.050
Companies blank publish their data; it is the law.

72
00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:20.922
A, are required to. B, need not. C, ought.

73
00:05:24.467 --> 00:05:26.954
The answer is A: are required to.

74
00:05:28.117 --> 00:05:30.569
Which expression means the action is optional?

75
00:05:30.869 --> 00:05:34.627
A, must not. B, need not. C, are required to.

76
00:05:38.167 --> 00:05:40.422
The answer is B: need not.

77
00:05:41.583 --> 00:05:43.432
Choose the correct sentence.

78
00:05:43.732 --> 00:05:51.623
A, Firms must not to share private data.. B, Firms must not share private data.. C, Firms must not sharing private data..

79
00:05:55.167 --> 00:05:59.186
The answer is B: Firms must not share private data..

80
00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:03.406
Being open about how a system works is called blank.

81
00:06:03.706 --> 00:06:07.568
A, transparency. B, tariff. C, debris.

82
00:06:11.133 --> 00:06:13.504
The answer is A: transparency.

83
00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:17.822
According to the reading, accountability fails if…

84
00:06:18.122 --> 00:06:23.331
A, rules differ in each nation. B, machines are slow. C, users read terms.

85
00:06:26.867 --> 00:06:29.923
The answer is A: rules differ in each nation.

86
00:06:31.133 --> 00:06:34.238
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:35.083 --> 00:06:38.430
Should there be global rules for artificial intelligence?

88
00:06:47.217 --> 00:06:48.775
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:49.075 --> 00:07:14.323
I think there should be at least basic global rules, because artificial intelligence crosses borders and its risks affect everyone. Common standards on transparency, safety testing and accountability would stop companies from choosing the weakest country. At the same time, the rules should remain flexible so that innovation can continue and local values can be respected.

90
00:07:14.623 --> 00:07:16.448
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:17.350 --> 00:07:23.578
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and obligation in rules: required to and must not and need not.

92
00:07:23.878 --> 00:07:29.027
Today's value is ethical responsibility. Take ownership of the effects of what you create.

93
00:07:29.327 --> 00:07:36.672
Congratulations! You have finished Online Newspaper Class, level four. See you in Online Newspaper Class, level five!

94
00:07:36.972 --> 00:07:40.170
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

